experiment: tutor
  instrument        tutor   trial seed 0000000001355bb9   1214 trials per creature
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)
  tutor -- can one creature teach another? Generation 0 is taught by the
  host, its produced vowels are measured, and generation 1 -- a DIFFERENT
  creature -- both HEARS those vowels and is REWARDED toward them.
  why voice    not genes: n_max re-rolls the wiring and appending re-rolls
               the noise, so a bred genome voids every anchor this project
               has. And transmission by voice composes two measured parts --
               repeats a heard word at 0.890, and hears its own voice.
  the quantity dF1: the difference between the formants a creature produces
               for the two words. The same number every naming result here
               is stated in.
  the control  `swap` gives generation 1 the SAME two tutor vowels with the
               word labels exchanged -- identical acoustics and spread,
               only the mapping destroyed. Beating it is the whole claim.
  refused if   tutored and swapped come back equal. Then the voice carries
               sound and not information, and culture does not start here.
  note         the tape carries FORMANTS only; f0 stays the host's, because
               the creature's own f0 is not recorded. A limit of the tape.
  both have    context_slots 2 and context_source 4 -- the DNA v53 derived
               index every dF1 here is measured under. The tutor needs it to
               HAVE a distinction and the pupil to be able to HOLD one, and
               source 4 derives the pupil's categories from what it HEARS,
               which makes the transmission real rather than nominal.


  WHAT THE TUTOR HANDS ON   its two produced vowels, geometric mean over
  its own scored trials:  670 +/- 5 Hz  and  573 +/- 4 Hz
  the host asked it for 780 and 320, a 460 Hz demand.

  generation                 dF1 (word0 - word1)             n
  0, taught by the host       +96.1 +/-   5.1 Hz           18
  1, tutored                  +25.6 +/-   7.6 Hz           18
  1, tutor vowels SWAPPED     -27.7 +/-   6.5 Hz           18
  1, tutor centre, LOUD       +61.0 +/-  11.3 Hz           18
  `loud` keeps the tutor's own vowel centre and widens the pair to the
  host's spread -- the tutor's voice with the contrast turned up. It is
  what separates a transmission failure from a distinction too small to
  learn, and those have opposite implications.

  DID EACH LESSON LAND   error early minus late
    generation 0  +0.1480 +/- 0.0053  (28.2 SE)
    generation 1  +0.0531 +/- 0.0119  (4.5 SE)

  TRANSMISSION   tutored minus swapped:  +53.2 +/- 10.3 Hz  (5.2 SE)
  the two arms differ ONLY in which tutor vowel goes with which word, so
  this is the information in the tutor's voice and not its sound.

  ONE CREATURE TAUGHT ANOTHER. The pupil's distinction follows the
  tutor's MAPPING and not merely its sound: +53.2 +/- 10.3 Hz over an
  arm with identical acoustics and the labels exchanged, 5.2 SE.
  Culture starts here -- a thing one creature learned reached a second
  through the air, with no genome passed and nothing bred.

  AND IT NARROWS. The tutor was asked for 460 Hz and produced +96.1; it
  handed on +25.6, which is 27% of what it got. Whether that ratio is
  stable across generations is what iterated learning is about and this
  run cannot say -- it measures ONE generation. A second generation is
  the same code with the tape taken from the pupil, and it is the next
  run rather than this one.
